status: done (2026-07-10). T1..T6 green at all layers.

T1 (scala unit) confirmed a query string with embedded double quotes and
underscores ("\"_ + _ = _\"") crosses tools/call -> backend.ir intact,
quote characters included; max_results plumbs through the same way as
sledgehammer's timeout_secs (json_args already omits the pair when the
client omits the argument, no special handler code needed).

T2 (ml unit) confirmed a malformed query (an unterminated quote) is a
clean status error from Find_Theorems.read_query, not a crash.

T3 (ml unit, pinned -- a genuine surprise): Find_Theorems.find_theorems_cmd
returns opt_found = NONE whenever a limit cuts the search short (the
exhaustive total is unknown at that point), so a BOUNDED query's tally
is "displaying N theorem(s)", never the optimistic "found N (M
displayed)" shape one might assume from ir.ML's own tally-formatting
code reading in isolation -- the found/displayed split only fires when
the search is unlimited (max_results 0) and completes exhaustively,
giving "found N theorem(s)" with N lines actually returned. Both shapes
are pinned in MCP_Repl_Tests.thy with "name:conj" (Main has more than
3 matches, so max_results=3 truncates and max_results=0 does not).

T4 (ml unit) confirmed goal-based criteria ("intro") error cleanly at
theory level (no goal to search from) and, once mid-proof on a
conjunction goal, return plausible rules including conjI.

T5 (e2e) confirmed the description's quoting contract is livable
without mcp_server.py's python-side auto-quoter: name:conjI (bare,
unquoted) and "_ + _ = _ + _" (quoted term pattern) both return hits
through the full stack -- the auto-quoting heuristic was deliberately
NOT reimplemented scala-side (recorded in the CHANGELOG).

T6 (bridge) confirmed find_theorems on one REPL returns promptly while
a slow 2s step is still in flight on a different REPL -- it needs no
async gymnastics of its own (unlike sledgehammer), but must not (and
does not) queue behind an unrelated slow call.

